{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:2f97d2ae7a609baf0dc70929bf9c559e63380bdba951685faf2f99b028d256f0","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.8},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.05}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"STEP 0 — ANCHOR. The question asks for a G7 GDP-weighted real yield, not US TIPS. PPP GDP weights (US 0.522, JP 0.115, DE 0.104, UK 0.077, FR 0.075, IT 0.062, CA 0.045; sums to 1.000) on current real 10y levels (US 2.60, JP 0.50, DE 0.90, UK 1.50, FR 1.20, IT 1.70, CA 1.70) = 1.357+0.058+0.094+0.116+0.090+0.105+0.077 = 1.896% -> ~1.90% today. Market-FX weights give ~2.05%. All three panelists reached the same conclusion (1.76 / 1.96 / 1.76); I confirm it independently and note that the Mechanism Modeler's weights SUM TO 1.03, so his \"1.96%\" is 6bp overstated — normalized it is 1.905%.\nSTEP 1 — r*, G7 PPP-weighted. Today: US ~0.9, JP ~0.0, euro ~0.3, UK ~0.6, CA ~0.8 -> weighted 0.62, call it 0.70. Deltas to 2032-36, in bp: life-cycle dissaving +22 (65+ hold ~40% of net worth, net worth/GDP ~4.5x = 1.8x GDP, decumulated at 1.2-1.5%/yr = -2.2 to -2.7% of GDP, against the 45-64 cohort's +1.8 to +2.2% -> net desired saving -0.4 to -0.9pp of GDP at a -35bp-per-pp loanable-funds slope); desired-wealth/longer-horizon -15; public dissaving FLOW +10 (the debt LEVEL belongs in the premium, not here); AI/grid/physical capex +5 (see the Fermi below); precautionary saving -15; cognitive-compression saving-glut-of-the-rich -4 (the Modeler booked -9 on AI-Q5 as a certainty; AI-Q5 is a 38% marginal, so the certainty-equivalent is -3 to -4); China pension-subsidy dissaving +8 (saving -1.5 to -2pp of China GDP x ~19% world weight = -0.3 to -0.4pp of world saving = +10 to +14bp, haircut because the household surplus persists if the backstop is not credible); Japan r* normalization +5. Sum = +11 to +16. r* 2032-36 = 0.85%, 1-sigma 0.40.\nAI/GRID CAPEX FERMI, because this arrow is the effort brief's own temptation and it does not survive arithmetic. AI-Q3 implies 998-365 = 633 TWh of incremental data-centre load by 2036 = 72 GW of average delivered power = ~96 GW of IT capacity. At $10-12M/MW all-in that is ~$1.06T over 11 years = $96B/yr, plus $35-45B/yr of generation and grid = ~$135B/yr total, which is 0.15% of bloc market-FX GDP and ~0.6% of bloc GFCF. At -35bp per pp of GDP, the whole AI complex is worth +5bp on r*, +15bp if you treble it for robots and non-DC physical AI. THE MECHANISM MODELER IS ARITHMETICALLY IMPOSSIBLE HERE: his +40bp requires 1.0-1.5pp of GDP of incremental investment demand, 7-10x what the LOCKED AI-Q3 marginal supports. The Resolution Auditor's +10-20bp is the defensible figure, though his itemization is wrong in the other direction — his $20-60B/yr covers grid only and omits the $96B/yr data-centre and chip stack, so his \"order of magnitude too small\" is really \"a factor of four too small.\" Both his number and his conclusion survive; his arithmetic does not.\nSTEP 2 — REAL TERM PREMIUM BY IDENTITY, NOT BY ASSERTION. Today's premium = today's yield minus today's r* = 1.90 - 0.70 = 1.20%. Closing the identity rather than estimating the premium independently is the whole point of my lens. Deltas: debt supply +33 (G7-weighted debt/GDP +15pp at 2.2bp/pp, Laubach's 3-4bp haircut for regulatory-captive domestic demand); convenience-yield erosion plus a fragile levered basis-trade marginal buyer plus reduced central-bank duration absorption +20; the closed-DB-scheme buyout channel that withdraws a price-insensitive long bid +10 (the Auditor's point, and arithmetically real); a decelerating China's lower global growth and inflation-volatility anchor -15. Premium, no-repression branch = 1.20+0.33+0.20+0.10-0.15 = 1.68%.\nSTEP 3 — THE CENTRAL FINDING: REPRESSION IS A BRANCH, NOT A SUBTRACTION. All three Wave-1 panelists ADD a +33 to +38bp debt-supply premium and then SUBTRACT an independent expected repression wedge (-20 / -33 / -55bp). That double-counts the same shock in opposite directions: repression is the policy response to, and the suppressor of, precisely the premium being netted against it, so the two terms are strongly negatively correlated and cannot be summed as independent components. The Japan 1990-2020 counterexample both outside-view panelists cite (debt 65->250% with FALLING real yields) does not refute the supply elasticity; it RELOCATES it into a repression branch. Treating it additively pulls the central down AND falsely narrows the distribution. Correct treatment is a two-branch mixture. Branch N (no material repression, p=0.60): yield = 0.85+1.68 = 2.53%, sigma 0.55. Branch R (repression materially binding for a sustained part of the window, p=0.40): the supply premium never clears into the market and an explicit wedge applies -> 0.85 + 1.05 - 0.80 = 1.10%, sigma 0.45; note repression's signature IS a low or negative real yield (G7 1945-80 ~0 to negative, Japan YCC 2016-24 real 10y ~-0.5 to 0), so it lands directly on this measure.\nMIXTURE MOMENTS. Mean = 0.40x1.10 + 0.60x2.53 = 0.440+1.518 = 1.958. Median by CDF: at 2.00, F = 0.40xPhi(2.00)+0.60xPhi(-0.964) = 0.391+0.101 = 0.492; at 2.05, F = 0.393+0.115 = 0.508 -> median ~2.02, shaded 5bp for the Auditor's net measurement artefact (his claimed +10bp cannot stand once his own swap-versus-breakeven item of -10 to -18bp on the aggregate is applied; the honest net is ~-5bp). P50 = 1.95. P10: at 0.80, F = 0.40xPhi(-0.667)+0.60x0.0008 = 0.101 -> P10 = 0.80. P90: at 3.05, F = 0.40 + 0.60xPhi(0.945) = 0.40+0.497 = 0.897 -> P90 = 3.05.\nVARIANCE ATTRIBUTION. Between-branch = p(1-p)(2.53-1.10)^2 = 0.24x2.045 = 0.491. Within-branch = 0.40x0.2025 + 0.60x0.3025 = 0.263. Total 0.753 (sd 0.868). So the REPRESSION BRANCH SWITCH carries 65% of my variance, the real term premium ~21%, r* ~14%. This reverses the Wave-1 panel's ordering (they all put 45-55% on the premium and 20-30% on repression) and it follows mechanically from framing repression as bimodal rather than additive.\nRESPECTING THE 2A (r-g) DISTINCTION, CHECKED ARITHMETICALLY. On my numbers r goes 1.90 -> 1.95 (+5bp) while g goes from ~2.7%/yr realized 2015-24 to 1.80%/yr (-90bp), so (r-g) worsens by ~+0.95pp — matching 2A's ~+1.0pp claim almost exactly, with r* contributing only +15bp of it. The claim is therefore fully satisfied by a near-flat r* plus fiscal arithmetic and premium, which is exactly the distinction the effort brief demands.\nPANELIST FLAG: the Reference-Class Scout's CQ4 is arithmetically impossible as stated. He builds a decomposition central of 1.0+0.9-0.5 = 1.4%, states a base-rate anchor of 2.0-2.1%, says he weights them 40/60, and reports 1.95%. 0.40x1.40 + 0.60x2.05 = 0.56+1.23 = 1.79%, not 1.95%. Reaching 1.95 from those two inputs needs ~17% weight on his own decomposition, not 40%. Separately, the Mechanism Modeler lists r* arrows of +35-20+12-9-15-10 = -7bp on a 0.90% base, which gives 0.83%, yet he reports 0.95% — a 12bp unexplained residual he attributes to Japanese normalization that his own weighted starting level already contains.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.743Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield (PPP-weighted, computed on the same seven benchmarks) averages below 1.00% for four consecutive quarters, with at least one G7 sovereign having publicly adopted explicit yield-curve control, a captive-buyer mandate for banks or insurers, or a formal duration-shortening program.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 repression branch has fired early and is binding on the G7-weighted measure, so its probability was well above 0.40. CQ4 P50 moves down from 1.95 to ~1.35 and P90 down from 3.05 to ~2.30; the variance attribution flips from 65% branch-switch to premium-dominated because the branch is resolved. CQ5 conjunct A falls (repression suppresses realized rate vol), taking CQ5 from 26 to ~17. CQ6-C gains ~5pp from B (repression runs inflation above nominal yields), and CQ3 loses ~0.10pp to misallocation.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative announced-and-cancelled or impaired hyperscaler AI buildout reaches 20% of announced capacity, OR net external AI software/API revenue stays below 0.5x annualized AI compute D&A for two consecutive reported years — i.e. AI-Q4 resolves YES — AND MSCI World does NOT fall 25% peak-to-trough within four quarters of the second confirming disclosure.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The bust happens without the equity event, which breaks the coupling my CQ5 conjunct B leans on: P(>=25% drawdown 2027-36) drops from 0.74 to ~0.55 and CQ5 falls from 26 to ~18. It also moves the bust INSIDE my CQ3 window as a pure investment drag without a demand collapse: CQ3 P50 down ~0.10 to 1.70, CQ6-D up ~4pp from B. If instead the drawdown DOES arrive with bonds rallying 100bp+, CQ5 falls further, to ~14.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD publishes a GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate for 2027-2029 that averages below 2.20% on the ALL-MEMBERS basis (Turkiye and Mexico included), not merely on an ex-Turkiye basis.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My Turkiye/Mexico weighting arithmetic — the single driver of my CQ2 being 4-19pp above my panel — has been overtaken by Turkish disinflation or a basket re-weighting. CQ2 falls from 37 to ~22, CQ6-C falls from 16 to ~11, CQ6-D rises from 11 to ~15, and the remaining ~1pp goes to B. My P(core 2032-36 >= 2.70) falls from 0.30 to ~0.18.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's published annual real GDP growth prints below 2.5% in any two consecutive calendar years, with the GDP deflator non-negative in both.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the 3.05%/yr published China path that supplies 1.22pp of my 1.80% coupled central, and removes the print-wedge cushion. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.80 to ~1.45 and P10 to ~0.70; CQ6 shifts to roughly A 6 / B 55 / C 15 / D 24, because the g<1.7 band grows and the bust branch dominates it. It also strengthens my CQ2 conjunct B (China deflation), raising CQ2 by ~2pp, and lowers r* by ~8bp via a persisting Chinese saving surplus, taking CQ4 P50 to ~1.85.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat SES 2030 wave (published ~2033) or UK ASHE and Canadian LFS annual medians show NACE F+Q real median hourly earnings outpacing J+K+M by more than 5.0pp cumulative over 2025-2030 in a majority of the members that publish both.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my quantified composition offsets (-12 to -16pp from Q entrants, +12 to +15pp from J/K/M truncation) that drive the central measured gap to -3pp. On a 5pp-by-2030 run rate the 10pp-by-2036 bar becomes reachable: P(CQ1 conjunct 2) rises from 0.10 to ~0.35 and CQ1 from 8 to ~22. It would also raise my care-wage pass-through assumption from 25-35% toward 50%, adding ~0.15pp to OECD core inflation and moving ~4pp of CQ6 mass from B into C.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 fermi seat, effort 'rstar'."}}